# How to measure a Darkbloom provider without fooling yourself

Method notes from running ~40 measured slots across five campaigns on one M4 Max.
`FINDINGS.md` records *what* we observed; this records *how to observe it* — and
mostly, the ways we got it wrong first. Every rule below was bought with a mistake.

If you take one thing: **demand variance on this network is larger than every
effect we set out to measure.** Almost all of the discipline here exists to stop
that single fact from producing confident nonsense.

---

## 1. Variance is the adversary. Budget rounds, not minutes

Measured per-slot revenue for **one model in one configuration**, 30-minute slots:

```
gemma-4-26b-qat-4bit, solo:  0.146  0.065  0.034  0.096  0.146  0.140  0.006  $/h
                                                   ... a 24x spread
```

Same machine, same model, same advertised set, nothing changed but the clock. Any
comparison that cannot survive a 24× swing in its inputs is not a comparison.

Consequences:

- **Four rounds per arm minimum.** Three is the floor for saying anything; four
  is where we stopped being surprised.
- **More rounds beats longer slots.** Restart count is fixed by `arms × rounds`,
  so lengthening slots buys sample size without reducing churn — but it does not
  attack variance, because a longer slot is still *one* draw from the demand
  distribution. Prefer 4×30min over 2×60min.
- **Report the spread, never just the mean.** With a handful of repeats a
  parametric confidence interval implies precision the design cannot support.
  Min/max across slots is honest; a ± figure is not.
- **Overlapping ranges mean "no result".** Two means differing by 15% with ranges
  spanning 4× is not a finding, however much you want it to be.

### The mistake, three times over

We announced conclusions from small n and had to retract each one:

| Claimed | On | Retracted because |
|---|---|---|
| "traffic contention is severe, net −30%" | 2 slots | 3rd slot came in high; real effect −1.4% |
| "gemma wins 2×, ranges don't overlap" | 2 slots/arm | later slots flipped the means |
| "qwen is the steadier earner" | 2 slots | its range widened to 7× |

Each time the caveat was stated *and the conclusion was still drawn*. Stating a
caveat does not license the claim. If n is too small, the output is "no result
yet" — say that instead.

---

## 2. Round-robin, never blocks

Run `A, B, C, A, B, C…`, not `AAA, BBB, CCC`. Demand varies by hour, so a block
design confounds "this model earns more" with "this model ran during the evening
peak". Round-robin gives every arm the same spread of clock times.

It works: in one run demand fell through the night (busy 0.70 → 0.60 → 0.28) and
every arm absorbed the decline roughly equally, so the *comparison* survived even
though no arm's absolute mean did.

Round-robin does **not** fix a monotone drift within a round, and it cannot rescue
an arm whose slots all failed. Check `busy_fraction` per slot before trusting a
round.

---

## 3. An arm is a set, and sets do not decompose

Measure the configuration you intend to run. Do not measure models individually
and add them up.

Measured directly: adding a second model to a machine **did not add revenue**.
Across three pairs, total $/h stayed in the same band as the best single model
while resident GB rose 55–137%. On one pair the sum of singles overstated the
measured pair by 17%; on another the pair was indistinguishable from the single.

If you must project an unmeasured set, label it an **upper bound** and say why.
`dbyield plan` emits `summed-singles-upper-bound` for exactly this and refuses to
call it a measurement.

### Split a pair's revenue by model before interpreting it

**Corrected 2026-09-27 — see `FINDINGS.md` §21.** This section used to read
"Co-advertised models starve, not share", on the grounds that in 5 of 6 paired slots the
second model received zero jobs (qwen3.6: 0/7/0/0; ternary-bonsai: 0), and concluded
"whatever wins the first races keeps winning". **That generalised from two second models
the network barely wants.** Across all 11 paired slots now on record:

| Second model | slots | its jobs | share of the arm |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| `ternary-bonsai-2-27b` | 4 | 0 | 0.0% |
| `qwen3.6-35b-a3b-vl-mtp-mxfp8` | 4 | 7 | 0.5% |
| `gpt-oss-20b` | 3 | 388 | **32.0%** |

`gpt-oss-20b` took work in all three of its slots and **out-earned gemma in one**. There is
no first-mover lock-in. Starvation tracks whether the network wants the second model, not
co-residency itself.

The practice the old rule implied is still right, and is the part to keep: **split a pair's
revenue by model before interpreting it.** If one side is at zero you have measured a single
model with extra RAM consumed, and should say so. If it is not at zero you are measuring a
genuine two-model configuration, and the memory-tax reading does not apply.

