## 4. Warm every arm, and verify residency — don't assume

A settle window excludes cold-start latency from the clock. It does **not** make
the model resident. Those are different things and only the second one matters,
because an unwarmed model can fail to attract any traffic at all, which reads as
"no demand".

The bias is *systematic*, not noise: in a round-robin the freshly switched arm is
the cold one, every slot.

Practice:
- Warm explicitly through the provider's loopback endpoint (`--local-endpoint`),
  which is guaranteed to hit this machine, is free, and never reaches the
  coordinator. `X-Darkbloom-Route: self` cannot target a machine and is not a
  substitute — see `FINDINGS.md` §10.
- **Verify** residency against the daemon afterwards. A successful warm request is
  evidence, not proof; the model can be evicted again.
- Record `warm_ok` per slot so a reader can discount the ones that failed.
- Note `startup_preload = true` often loads models anyway, which makes warming
  belt-and-braces rather than load-bearing. That is a reason to keep it, not to
  skip it: when it is redundant it costs 7 seconds.

---

## 5. Verify the switch actually happened

Read the advertised set back from the daemon after applying it. Do not trust the
exit status of the command that applied it.

An early version wrote `provider.toml` and restarted; launchd relaunched from the
plist's stale `--model` flags, which win. Every slot reported `restart_ok: true`
while advertising the wrong models. The data looked perfect and was worthless.

`advertised_verified` is now recorded per slot, and a mismatch aborts the run
rather than producing attributable-looking garbage.

---

## 6. The numerator and denominator must cover the same clock

Revenue may only be divided by exposure that was actually observed. Dividing days
of accumulated payouts by minutes of recorded sampling produced **$4.22/hour and
126,000 jobs/hour** in development.

Record the exact intervals you sampled, count only payouts inside them, and report
revenue from outside them separately — never divided. Gaps (sampler down, Mac
asleep, daemon stopped) are not exposure and must not be credited to any model.

---

## 7. Record the conditions, not just the outcome

Revenue alone cannot distinguish "this model earns less" from "this slot was dealt
a worse hand". Per slot, capture at minimum:

| Covariate | Separates |
|---|---|
| `busy_fraction` | idle machine vs. busy-but-unprofitable |
| `jobs_per_hour` and `$/job` | low volume vs. low value — they move independently |
| input:output ratio, p50/p90 completion length | a generous hour from a better model |
| concurrency in-flight / queued vs. cap | demand ceiling vs. **our own** ceiling |
| KV grant per model | the co-residency penalty, directly |
| competitors advertising/warm on each model | a crowded market vs. a bad model |
| coordinator-side request counters | routed volume, immune to local restarts |

Two of these changed conclusions outright. Per-job value revealed that qwen3.6 is
a low-volume/high-value model (2.6× revenue per job, 3.7× fewer jobs) rather than
simply worse. Concurrency showed we were never above 4 of 8 in-flight, which
retired a whole planned experiment.

---

## 8. Instrument before you perturb

We planned to discover whether we were concurrency-limited by *lowering* the cap
until revenue moved — spending real revenue to locate a knee. Then we found the
provider already exposes `kv_active_requests` and `kv_waiting_requests` on a
loopback Prometheus endpoint. Reading the number cost nothing and answered the
question outright.

Before designing an experiment that perturbs a live earning system, spend an hour
looking for the counter that already exists. Ask of any planned treatment: *is
this observable instead?*

Corollary: verify a treatment reaches the thing you think it reaches. We nearly
built a memory-headroom experiment on filler RAM, having assumed it would move the
number the router reads. It does not — that number is the provider's own in-process
MLX accounting, untouchable from outside. `dbyield memory-probe` exists to check
the mechanism *before* the campaign.

---

## 9. A perfectly healthy provider can earn exactly zero

Twice, for 30 minutes each: connected, trusted, challenge fresh, advertised set
correct, model resident, 40 GB KV grant, zero errors, **zero log output**, and the
coordinator's own counter for us did not move by one. Serving 400–500 requests per
half hour immediately before and after.

Nothing local indicated a problem. `darkbloom status` was clean. `inference_active
= false` for 30 minutes is indistinguishable from a quiet hour unless you know what
the rest of the fleet was doing.

This matters twice over:

- **Operationally**, it is the worst failure class — no alarm, no symptom, just
  absent money. Detect it: advertised + resident + coordinator counter flat +
  *fleet still busy* ⇒ alert. That last clause is what stops it firing at 4am when
  zero traffic is correct. (`dbyield watchdog`.)
- **Statistically**, such slots are not measurements of the arm. How you treat them
  swings a conclusion: including gemma's two zeros put it level with qwen3.6;
  excluding them put it 50% ahead. **Report both numbers** and say which question
  each answers — "what does this model yield when routing works" and "what does
  this configuration yield in practice" are different questions, and only the
  second should include stalls.

Decide the validity rule *before* seeing the data, and record enough per slot to
apply it afterwards.

---

## 10. Leave a guard gap between arms

Coordinator-side fault state deliberately survives a provider reconnect. Several
trackers run on roughly five-minute clocks, so a 30-minute slot cadence can measure
arm N+1 while arm N's penalties still apply.

We use a 6-minute settle window, sampled but not measured, which doubles as where
the warm-up load is paid. Cheap insurance; the alternative is longer slots.

(The specific TTLs behind "six minutes" came from source we later found to be
stale — see §12. The practice is still sound on general grounds: cooldowns exist,
they are not instant, and a guard gap costs one slot's worth of sampling.)

---

## 11. Never let instrumentation break the experiment

Best-effort telemetry must not be able to kill a run — and above all must not be
able to skip cleanup.

A timeout reading an HTTP response *body* surfaced as `TimeoutError`, escaped an
`except RuntimeError`, and killed a 4.8-hour experiment two minutes in. Worse, one
of those handlers sat inside the `finally` block, so the exception aborted cleanup
before the provider's model set was restored. It was harmless only by luck: the
restore target happened to match the running arm.

Rules:
- Wrap every optional data source so it returns a value or a reason, never raises.
- Guard **each** cleanup step independently. The restore is a promise; nothing
  before it may throw and nothing after it may be skipped because something did.
- If restore fails anyway, print the exact command to fix it by hand.
- Check that a launched run is a *running* run. We lost 1.9 hours to a dead
  process because "it started" was taken as "it is working".

---

## 12. Verify the provenance of any mechanism you quote

We explained several findings by quoting Darkbloom's source, from a checkout that
turned out to be **ten days old, on a fork branch, five releases behind the
running provider** (`0.9.4` constants vs `0.9.9` live). A `deploy/environments/*.env`
in such a tree is not the deployed configuration.

`git log -1` and one version-constant check would have caught it in seconds. Do
both before quoting a coefficient, a gate, or an env default.

**And read the file's own header before citing it as evidence.** We twice quoted
`deploy/environments/prod.env` as proof of deployed configuration. Its first
paragraph says it "is not consumed directly" and that the authoritative runtime file
is elsewhere, with operator overrides preserved — so it shows release defaults and
silently diverges from production by design. A checked-in env file is almost never
the running config; treat one as a default until someone reads the live value.

**And when a measurement contradicts a document, the measurement wins.** We read a
rule saying gemma-4 may only route to machines advertising nothing else, called it
the explanation for co-advertised starvation, and published it — while holding our
own data showing gemma earning 401, 502 and 331 jobs on a mixed box, which the
rule forbids. The contradiction was noticed, labelled a "loose end", and reasoned
past. That is backwards. A primary observation that refutes a hypothesis outranks
a config file, and outranks wanting the explanation to be true.

---

## 13. Make the data say what it is

Small representational choices decide whether a report misleads:

- **Mark dry runs.** A rehearsal that writes `applied: true` is indistinguishable
  from a real run in the archive. Mark it in the record *and* in the report.
- **Never print `0` for "not measured".** A measured zero and an absent
  measurement are opposite decisions. Print `—`.
- **Record absence explicitly.** Missing concurrency samples and genuinely zero
  concurrency look identical unless the gap is counted.
- **Name the basis of every derived number** (`measured`, `measured-set`,
  `summed-singles-upper-bound`, `ledger-span`). A reader cannot discount what is
  not labelled.
- **A diagnostic that names the wrong cause is worse than none.** Ours reported
  "the provider has never been started with `--local-endpoint`" three times while
  the flag was plainly set and the listener had merely failed to bind. Each time
  it sent us to the wrong place.

---

## 14. Pre-flight checklist for a run worth running

- [ ] Sampler already running, with ≥2 h of history
- [ ] No other controller managing model selection (`foreign_change()` clean)
- [ ] Every arm's models downloaded, catalog-active, and within the memory budget
- [ ] ≥4 rounds, ≥10-minute slots, round-robin order
- [ ] Settle window ≥ the longest cooldown you know of
- [ ] Warming available and verified, or the run explicitly marked unwarmed
- [ ] A shared reference arm if results must join an earlier campaign
- [ ] Restore target confirmed, and config backed up
- [ ] Watchdog running, so a stall is caught live rather than reconstructed
- [ ] Validity rule for a slot written down **before** looking at results

---

## 15. Reporting honestly

- Lead with n. "0.12 $/h across 2 slots" and "0.12 $/h across 8" are different
  claims.
- Give the range beside every mean.
- Quantify what you excluded and why, and show the result both ways when the
  choice is arguable.
- Separate measurement from mechanism. Ours are in different documents for this
  reason: `FINDINGS.md` §0 marks which claims rest on unverified source.
- Retract plainly. Correct the claim, correct the artefacts that repeated it, and
  state what the evidence actually supports — without relitigating.
